.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAD 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ead \- read the Euclid audit trail
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B ead
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ead
module reads the audit trail: what was run, by whom, in which account and
namespace, with the parameters the command carried and the status it was
answered with.
.SH IT DOES NOT WRITE THE TRAIL
Every module records its own commands as it answers them, in the one
place every action of every module passes through. EAD only reads.
.PP
That split is the point. An audit whose write path ran through a separate
module would be missing entries exactly when that module was down or
stopped \- which is both the likeliest moment for something worth
auditing and the easiest thing for somebody to arrange. Stopping
.B ead
stops people reading the trail; it does not stop the trail.
.SH WHAT IS IN IT
Everything that changed something, and everything that was refused or
failed whatever its kind.
.PP
The refusals are why this is an audit rather than a log of activity. A
delete somebody was not allowed to make is a 403, and a 403 that is not
recorded looks exactly like a delete nobody ever attempted.
.PP
The monitoring module is left out entirely, whatever the action and
whatever the status. Every instance of every module pushes to
.B emo
on a timer, and none of its actions read as reads \- so without this the
machinery would be most of the trail. The document store
.B emd
is out for the same reason, being underneath every read and write euclid
makes. A metrics push that starts failing fails on a timer too, so these
stay out even when they are refused; the module log is where that
belongs.
.PP
Successful reads are left out by default. On a working installation they
outnumber everything else by orders of magnitude \- one parse run is
millions of
.I esm:get-object
calls \- and a trail that large buries what it was kept for. Set
.I euclid.modules.ead.audit-reads
to include them.
.SH PARAMETERS ARE REDACTED
A request body is where passwords, secrets, tokens and private keys
travel, and an audit log is read widely and exported freely \- that is
what it is for. So sensitive values are replaced with
.I ***
before the entry is ever written, on the writing side: there is no call
that stores a raw body, and nothing to remember to switch on.
.PP
The field name is kept and only the value replaced, so the trail still
says
.I that
a password was set, by whom and on which user, without saying what it was
set to. That is the auditable fact; the value never was.
.PP
Matching is on substrings of field names, without regard to case, and it
descends into nested objects and arrays. A body that is not JSON has no
field structure to judge, so it is recorded as its size alone rather than
guessed at.
.SH RETENTION
Each entry is written with an expiry taken from
.IR euclid.modules.ead.retention ,
in days, and the database removes it when it comes. Zero keeps entries
for ever, and then no expiry is written at all \- so turning retention on
later sweeps only what was written after it, never the existing trail.
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-ead-purge-events (1)
is for the installation that changed its mind and needs a window gone
now.
.SH SCOPE
Every read is confined to the caller's own account, whatever the filter
asks for. An audit that let one account read another's trail would hand
over the shape of somebody else's installation \- which buckets they
keep, who works there, what they do \- through the one module built to be
read by people looking for exactly that.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B list-events
One page of the trail, newest first. See
.BR euclid-cli-ead-list-events (1).
.TP
.B count-events
How many entries a filter matches, without listing them. See
.BR euclid-cli-ead-count-events (1).
.TP
.B purge-events
Removes entries older than a given age. See
.BR euclid-cli-ead-purge-events (1).
.SH AUTHENTICATION
All
.B ead
commands require the caller to be authenticated; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
first. Reading the trail needs
.I ead:list-events
and
.IR ead:count-events ,
which the built-in
.I reader
and
.I operator
roles hold. Purging needs
.IR ead:purge-events ,
which only
.I account-administrator
holds \- removing audit records is not an operator's job.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# What has happened recently
euclid-cli ead list-events

# What one person did
euclid-cli ead list-events --user jvo --page-size 50

# Every bucket deletion, whoever made it
euclid-cli ead list-events --module esm --command delete-bucket

# How much the trail holds for one module
euclid-cli ead count-events --module eqs
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ead-list-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ead-count-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ead-purge-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1)
